  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is zero prior information about which modality, encoder, profile setting, or aggregation strategy is most important. The most critical uncertainty is whether the RNA signal alone (with the simplest possible encoder) provides a meaningful performance floor. 2) What this tests: config_001 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) is the minimal baseline—simplest encoder (PCA), simplest profile (single), single modality. It establishes the performance reference point against which every other axis (encoder complexity, multiprofile, image modality, aggregation, fusion) can be measured. 3) Why it is the best first use of budget: As the first experiment, it maximizes information per trial by anchoring the entire search space. Every subsequent comparison (e.g., config_002 tests multiprofile+avgpool vs. this baseline; config_004 tests scvi vs. PCA; config_016 tests adding image) isolates exactly one or two design decisions relative to this reference. Starting with a more complex configuration would confound multiple variables and make it impossible to attribute performance gains to specific choices under the limited budget."
